WEBVTT


00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.730
There are two commonly used
print features in DesignaKnit.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.520
The text format and the
garment notation format.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.650
Many machine knitters find the
text format difficult to follow.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.820
With a little extra effort.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.120
You can create your own
cheat sheet following the

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.040
garment notation instructions.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.160
The cheat sheet will make it so much
easier to follow along as you're knitting.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:34.780
If you are new to knitting and garment
notation, this format is also called

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.860
the Japanese notation format, and
it may seem a bit cryptic, but I

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:45.080
encourage you to investigate using
it and add some of your own notes.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.430
Let's look at a simple shaped sleeve.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.310
The text instructions are detailed,
but they aren't visually easy to use.

00:00:56.595 --> 00:01:01.995
Let's translate the Japanese
instructions and make them easy for you.

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:07.905
Your instructions here in DesignaKnit
may look different than mine.

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:11.205
I formatted them the way I like them.

00:01:11.595 --> 00:01:16.455
Please check the tutorial "Japanese
Notation Format" for adjusting how

00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:18.345
your instructions are displayed.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:24.830
I'm using a spreadsheet for the math,
but the goal here is to make just a

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.570
little table for yourself to follow
each of the sets of instructions.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.150
On row zero,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.370
I'll start with 64 stitches.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.580
Starting at row 3, I'll increase
one stitch on each side and

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.890
continue to increase on both sides.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.750
Every 3 rows, a total of 15 times.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.690
Now, I don't know about you,
but I'm pretty easily distracted

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:56.410
and I like to keep track of my
stitch count as I'm knitting.

00:01:57.429 --> 00:02:01.449
What's missing in these instructions
are the stitch counts as you go along,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.210
so add them to your cheat sheet.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.840
Now if you get interrupted, it's easier
to know that it's time to increase or fix

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:16.900
an increase that you may have missed and
still keep your stitch count accurate.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:27.600
At row 48 I'll also increase on
each side and repeat these increases

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:36.840
every 4th row a total of 20 times
on row 128 you're instructed to

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.150
increase again just one time.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.040
Finally, we're instructed to knit
to row 137 with no increases.

00:02:46.545 --> 00:02:50.515
And if we paid attention,
we'll end up with 136 stitches.

00:02:52.185 --> 00:02:55.035
Again, I used a spreadsheet for the math.

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:59.955
For something easy like this, you might
just use a pencil and make some notes

00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:02.385
along these Japanese instructions.

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:10.485
The point is to use these Japanese
notation garment notation format and

00:03:10.485 --> 00:03:13.125
make notes for yourself to stay on track.

